  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThis morning...Outflow from a collapsed MCS (Mesoscale Convective\nSystem) over western AZ is moving westward across Riverside County\nand producing isolated, weak tstms. These storms will likely \ncollapse before reaching western Riverside or San Bernardino \nCounties but there is a small chance that they could reach the \nCoachella Valley intact by 3 am. If any storms reach western \nRiverside County, they will likely be quite weak. Although locally\ngusty winds could still be produced. The marine layer remains \nquite shallow and low clouds are slow to develop. Low cloud \ndevelopment could be further disrupted as the outflow from the MCS\nover AZ moves west of the mtns, although the outflow will be much\nweakened as it moves westward.\n\nThe high pressure over the southwest will continue to dominate our\nweather through Tuesday even as it weakens in response to a series\nof shortwave troughs moving inland to the north. The position of\nthe high will allow the monsoonal flow to continue bringing deep-\nlayer moisture into SoCal from the southeast. This will continue\nto support the potential for afternoon/evening showers and tstms,\nmainly in the mtns and deserts. Daytime high temperatures will\ngradually lower through the week but remain above normal through\nMonday. Today, we will still have minor HeatRisk in the inland \nvalleys and mtns, and major HeatRisk in the deserts. By Tuesday, \ndaytime high temperatures will be near or a few degrees below \nnormal. The marine layer will gradually deepen as the high aloft \nweakens.\n\nFor Wed through next weekend (according to model consensus)...a\ntrough of low pressure deepens along the coast. This will bring\nmore pronounced cooling, with daytime high temperatures as much as\n10 degrees below normal in some inland areas by Thursday. The\nbelow-normal temperatures will continue into next weekend. The\ntrough will also bring dry, southwest flow aloft, reducing the\npotential for pm showers/tstms by Wed and essentially eliminating\nit for Thursday through next weekend. In addition, the marine\nlayer will deepen and low clouds and fog could spread inland to\nthe coastal slopes during the nights and mornings.",
